system "ISO:tha-Thai:Latn:11940-1998" {

metadata {
  authority_id iso
  id 11940-1998
  language iso-639-2:tha
  source_script Thai
  destination_script Latn
  name ISO 11940:1998 Information and documentation -- Transliteration of Thai
  url https://www.iso.org/standard/20574.html
  creation_date 1998
  adoption_date 
  description 

  notes {
    note "Encoded from the Wikipedia presentation of ISO 11940
    (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_11940, CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved
    2026-11-19) as the interim working source; the official ISO
    11940:1998 document remains the purchase of record for provenance."
    note "The standard distinguishes the abbreviation symbol paiyannoi
    from the sentence terminator angkhandiao (transliterated ǂ and
    ǀ), but TIS 620-2533 and Unicode encode both uses as U+0E2F; the
    map carries the paiyannoi reading ǂ. The ICU/CLDR implementation
    substitutes a double dagger for both uses."
    note "ICU/CLDR divergences from ISO 11940 are not reproduced: the
    horn is U+031B, not a prime (ฅ ฒ ษ); ฏ is ṭ, not t̩; ำ is å, not
    ả; phinthu U+0E3A is ring below U+0325, not ˌ; ฯ is ǂ, not ‡;
    ๚ is ǁ, not ||. Preposed vowels are not transposed, per the
    standard's recommendation: เชียงใหม่ transliterates
    echīyngıh̄m̀, not the ICU form cheīyngh̄ım̀."
    note "Character sequencing (clause 4.2): left to right, and top to
    bottom at the same horizontal position, with tone marks and
    thanthakhat preceding any other marks attached to a consonant.
    The parallel substitution preserves Thai storage order, so marks
    stacked on one consonant are emitted in stored order; a stateless
    map cannot reorder a stack into the clause's tone-marks-first
    sequence. The standard denies that sara u and nikkhahit can
    combine; stored together they emit u plus ring above in stored
    order."
    note "Consonant diacritics follow the standard's entry order -
    base letter, macron, then dot below, horn, or macron below -
    and compose normalises each output to canonical (NFC) form
    where a precomposed character exists (e.g. k with dot below as
    U+1E33). U+0E45 (isolated lakkhangyao) transliterates ɨ per the
    standard's provision, which notes the isolated letter should not
    occur. U+0E3F (baht) is outside the working source's tables and
    passes through unchanged."
  }
}

tests {
   "ภาษาไทย" ->  "p̣hās̛̄āịthy"
   "เชียงใหม่" ->  "echīyngıh̄m̀"

# Exhaustive per-row tests for iso-tha-Thai-Latn-11940-1998, generated from the source
# document's table.
# Source: ISO 11940:1998 Thai transliteration, all tables. Working source:
# the Wikipedia presentation "ISO 11940"
# (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_11940), CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved
# 2026-11-19; used as the interim source pending acquisition of the official
# ISO 11940:1998 document, which remains the purchase of record for
# provenance. Rows in document order: the 46-consonant presentation table
# (44 consonant letters plus the ฤ/ฦ rows), the vowel-sign table (repeated ฤ
# ฦ ย ว อ entries deduplicated; ฤๅ/ฦๅ encoded as the constituent pairs), the
# isolated-lakkhangyao provision (ɨ), the other-combining-marks table
# (phinthu as ring below per the ISO column; the ICU CLDR implementation's ˌ
# is a recorded divergence), and the punctuation-and-digits table (paiyannoi
# reading ǂ for U+0E2F; the standard's angkhandiao reading ǀ is
# unrepresentable because TIS 620/Unicode do not distinguish the two uses).
# Expected strings are the post-compose (NFC where a precomposed character
# exists) forms. Excludes U+0E3F baht, which the working source's tables do
